                        "language": "english",
                        "review": "Ori and the Blind Forest is one of the best modern metroidvanias out there, and one of those rare games that stays with you long after the credits roll.\n\nThe world of Nibel is a joy to explore. Every area feels distinct; from the glowing, misty depths of Ginso Tree to the cold, crystalline heights of Mount Horu and and each is hand painted with a level of detail that makes you stop just to take it all in. It’s easily one of the most beautiful games I’ve played.\n\nThe gameplay more than holds up its end. Platforming is tight and responsive and new abilities keep opening up the world in satisfying ways, exactly what you want from a metroidvania. The escape sequences are tense and the challenge feels fair, rewarding skill without ever becoming frustrating.\n\nThe soundtrack is stunning. The orchestral score swells at exactly the right moments and elevates every scene, whether it’s quiet and reflective or building to a heart pounding climax. I listen to it outside the game regularly.\n\nThe story is simple on the surface but it hits surprisingly hard. The opening alone is enough to tug at the heartstrings and the emotional beats that follow are earned and beautifully handled.\n\nWhether you’re a genre veteran or new to metroidvanias this is a must play. A true work of art and a modern staple of the genre.",
